WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.981 --> 00:00:05.963
Laws have been translated into
software by hand for decades.

2
00:00:05.963 --> 00:00:10.846
Every implementing organisation has its
own interpretation of law and regulation,

3
00:00:10.846 --> 00:00:13.955
and a large part of it
ends up in software.

4
00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:19.263
This has been going on for
years, sometimes decades,

5
00:00:19.263 --> 00:00:23.085
and statutory amendments and new bills

6
00:00:23.085 --> 00:00:24.826
end up piled on top of
each other in software.

7
00:00:24.826 --> 00:00:27.327
The result: there is no overview any more.

8
00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:30.648
It is unclear which piece of software
belongs to which piece of legislation or

9
00:00:30.648 --> 00:00:33.450
regulation, and vice versa.

10
00:00:33.450 --> 00:00:37.951
So how that software works
becomes unpredictable.

11
00:00:37.951 --> 00:00:42.393
Not just for citizens and
businesses on the receiving end,

12
00:00:42.393 --> 00:00:47.113
but also for policymakers, civil servants

13
00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:52.154
and even for IT specialists

14
00:00:52.154 --> 00:00:54.554
who build the software.

15
00:00:54.554 --> 00:01:00.276
Making changes becomes
unmanageable: people no longer dare,

16
00:01:00.276 --> 00:01:03.997
because they no longer know

17
00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:06.497
the effect of a change.

18
00:01:06.497 --> 00:01:10.658
RegelRecht asks: what if
we start with the law,

19
00:01:10.658 --> 00:01:14.676
make the law itself machine-executable
and publish it openly?

20
00:01:14.676 --> 00:01:20.598
I am Anne Schuth, staff
engineer at RegelRecht,

21
00:01:20.598 --> 00:01:23.700
and I am giving the demo for
the next quarter of an hour.

22
00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:25.180
This is not a static demo.

23
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:30.462
You can stop at any time
and click around yourself.

24
00:01:30.462 --> 00:01:34.544
Questions I often get appear on the left.

25
00:01:34.544 --> 00:01:37.059
Click on them for answers.

26
00:01:37.059 --> 00:01:38.560
This is a complete story.

27
00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.141
It is working software,
but not a finished product.

28
00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.392
It is not meant to go into production.

29
00:01:45.392 --> 00:01:48.374
We do this from a policy directorate:

30
00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:49.715
we make policy by doing.

31
00:01:49.715 --> 00:01:54.678
We start at the very end, with a rulework,

32
00:01:54.678 --> 00:01:55.839
in this case for the healthcare allowance.

33
00:01:55.839 --> 00:02:00.022
This is a machine-executable
version of the healthcare allowance.

34
00:02:00.022 --> 00:02:03.864
Not the whole law, only
article 2 and article 4.

35
00:02:03.864 --> 00:02:08.467
Article 2: the full text is here.

36
00:02:08.467 --> 00:02:12.390
Below is the machine-executable
part of the law.

37
00:02:13.395 --> 00:02:15.016
That is an interpretation of that law.

38
00:02:15.016 --> 00:02:19.478
It starts with a parameter: BSN.

39
00:02:19.478 --> 00:02:22.860
Actions hang off it: does
someone meet the conditions?

40
00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:28.083
Someone must be at least the
minimum age and be insured.

41
00:02:28.083 --> 00:02:30.485
Then comes the amount of
the healthcare allowance.

42
00:02:30.485 --> 00:02:33.026
That is a whole calculation,
I won't go into detail.

43
00:02:33.026 --> 00:02:36.408
Below you see Operation Add:
adding income to partner income,

44
00:02:36.408 --> 00:02:41.016
so it is comparable to the threshold
income for an allowance partner.

45
00:02:41.016 --> 00:02:45.941
What I skipped over, we call inputs.

46
00:02:45.941 --> 00:02:49.465
Those are references to other laws.

47
00:02:49.465 --> 00:02:53.349
For example "is insured",
one of the conditions.

48
00:02:53.349 --> 00:02:54.229
That comes from another law.

49
00:02:54.229 --> 00:02:58.053
The healthcare allowance act says: insured
person, as meant in the Health Insurance Act.

50
00:02:58.053 --> 00:03:01.575
We click through to the
Health Insurance Act

51
00:03:01.575 --> 00:03:03.676
and see what it means to be insured.

52
00:03:03.676 --> 00:03:07.418
Someone is insured, which is
an output here, if they have a

53
00:03:07.418 --> 00:03:13.221
insurance policy or treaty
insurance and are not in detention.

54
00:03:13.221 --> 00:03:16.382
"In detention" is again an input, from the

55
00:03:16.382 --> 00:03:18.535
Penitentiary Principles Act.

56
00:03:18.535 --> 00:03:22.437
We'll go there and see what being detained

57
00:03:22.437 --> 00:03:22.737
means.

58
00:03:22.737 --> 00:03:27.960
You are in a penal institution
or remand centre and you

59
00:03:27.960 --> 00:03:29.881
are confined or temporarily absent.

60
00:03:29.881 --> 00:03:31.842
So you see: to

61
00:03:31.842 --> 00:03:35.924
compute the healthcare allowance,
the entitlement and the amount,

62
00:03:35.924 --> 00:03:40.047
you need a whole series of laws.

63
00:03:40.047 --> 00:03:42.308
Actually, it is not a series, but a graph.

64
00:03:42.308 --> 00:03:44.629
If you make laws machine-executable,

65
00:03:44.649 --> 00:03:46.333
you can visualise that.

66
00:03:46.333 --> 00:03:48.715
Then you see that the laws are connected.

67
00:03:49.576 --> 00:03:53.379
I'll zoom in a bit. Here you see

68
00:03:54.560 --> 00:03:57.282
the healthcare allowance.

69
00:03:57.282 --> 00:04:02.567
Here is "is insured";
we'll follow that line

70
00:04:02.567 --> 00:04:03.568
further.

71
00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:06.510
We arrive at the Health Insurance
Act, where there is "detained",

72
00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.412
and we follow that line too.

73
00:04:08.412 --> 00:04:10.655
We see that here.

74
00:04:10.655 --> 00:04:13.567
This is where the detention
status is determined.

75
00:04:14.906 --> 00:04:18.749
Here you see the whole system of
laws and regulations that is needed

76
00:04:18.749 --> 00:04:20.691
to arrive at the healthcare allowance.

77
00:04:20.691 --> 00:04:24.854
If I click this, you see all the
legislation and regulations we have in the

78
00:04:24.854 --> 00:04:26.776
proof of concept right now.

79
00:04:26.776 --> 00:04:29.858
That's quite a list.

80
00:04:29.858 --> 00:04:32.921
Guess which one this might be.

81
00:04:33.847 --> 00:04:35.289
Feel free to look around yourself.

82
00:04:37.671 --> 00:04:43.678
Click around, zoom in and
out and follow lines to

83
00:04:43.678 --> 00:04:47.983
see the coherence between
laws and regulations.

84
00:04:47.983 --> 00:04:49.594
We'll continue shortly.

85
00:04:49.594 --> 00:04:54.117
A question I often get:

86
00:04:55.778 --> 00:04:59.941
how do we deal with ambiguity?

87
00:04:59.941 --> 00:05:02.383
Say the network contains

88
00:05:02.383 --> 00:05:08.187
two definitions with the same name,

89
00:05:08.187 --> 00:05:13.071
such as partner income or household.

90
00:05:14.288 --> 00:05:19.115
That really happens.
You can search for it.

91
00:05:19.115 --> 00:05:22.416
In different laws, partner income

92
00:05:22.416 --> 00:05:26.634
or household is defined differently.

93
00:05:28.163 --> 00:05:31.303
We humans find that annoying.

94
00:05:31.303 --> 00:05:33.884
For machines it makes no difference,

95
00:05:33.884 --> 00:05:35.992
as long as you point to
the right definition.

96
00:05:35.992 --> 00:05:41.640
This can be generated automatically

97
00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:45.561
once the law is machine-executable.

98
00:05:45.561 --> 00:05:50.282
That is useful for legal analysts,

99
00:05:50.282 --> 00:05:55.424
legislative lawyers and others who
work with laws and regulations.

100
00:05:55.424 --> 00:06:00.505
It shows what is connected and what

101
00:06:00.505 --> 00:06:03.046
might break when you change something.

102
00:06:04.761 --> 00:06:08.522
If the law is machine-executable,

103
00:06:08.522 --> 00:06:09.403
then the following is also possible.

104
00:06:09.403 --> 00:06:14.244
Here we look at scenarios, cases.

105
00:06:14.244 --> 00:06:18.026
These are often described

106
00:06:19.628 --> 00:06:22.290
in the explanatory memorandum.

107
00:06:22.290 --> 00:06:25.933
A bill or amendment to a law goes

108
00:06:25.933 --> 00:06:29.075
to the House, usually with an explanatory

109
00:06:29.075 --> 00:06:31.156
memorandum.

110
00:06:31.156 --> 00:06:33.798
It sets out how the law should work

111
00:06:33.798 --> 00:06:36.440
in specific cases.

112
00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:42.104
Sometimes there is a table with properties

113
00:06:42.104 --> 00:06:45.867
of people, households or businesses.

114
00:06:45.867 --> 00:06:48.690
That is how the memorandum
shows the intent.

115
00:06:48.690 --> 00:06:52.545
You take such a case from
the explanatory memorandum.

116
00:06:53.476 --> 00:06:54.676
This is what that looks like, for example.

117
00:06:54.676 --> 00:06:59.618
A person with a fictitious
BSN and fictitious wage.

118
00:06:59.618 --> 00:07:04.020
If the healthcare allowance
act is run, this is the

119
00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:07.881
healthcare allowance
in eurocents per year.

120
00:07:07.881 --> 00:07:11.142
That is why it is called
machine-executable law-

121
00:07:11.142 --> 00:07:13.403
and regulation: we can run it.

122
00:07:13.403 --> 00:07:19.085
You get a trace of the execution of

123
00:07:19.085 --> 00:07:20.205
the law on this specific case.

124
00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:23.746
Here the healthcare allowance act is run,

125
00:07:23.746 --> 00:07:27.510
to determine the amount of
the healthcare allowance

126
00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.731
for the BSN from just now.

127
00:07:28.731 --> 00:07:32.134
A little lower is the
Health Insurance Act,

128
00:07:32.134 --> 00:07:35.518
executed: is someone insured?

129
00:07:35.518 --> 00:07:38.320
A bit lower still: the Penitentiary

130
00:07:38.320 --> 00:07:41.583
Principles Act, for whether
someone is detained.

131
00:07:41.583 --> 00:07:45.227
This person is not detained.

132
00:07:45.227 --> 00:07:46.528
So it simply carries on.

133
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:51.525
At the very bottom,
after a lot of scrolling,

134
00:07:51.525 --> 00:07:56.532
is the healthcare allowance
in euro cents per year,

135
00:07:56.532 --> 00:08:00.658
with what we saw in this case.

136
00:08:04.805 --> 00:08:07.950
Someone under 18 should have no
right to a healthcare allowance.

137
00:08:07.950 --> 00:08:10.273
This is another scenario.

138
00:08:10.273 --> 00:08:14.079
If we run this, the trace has become a lot

139
00:08:14.079 --> 00:08:15.081
shorter.

140
00:08:15.081 --> 00:08:18.686
This person is 17, and that means

141
00:08:19.847 --> 00:08:20.668
it goes no further.

142
00:08:20.668 --> 00:08:21.989
That was one of the conditions.

143
00:08:21.989 --> 00:08:25.771
You had to be at least
the minimum age: 18.

144
00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:26.912
That's where it stops.

145
00:08:26.912 --> 00:08:32.195
This person does not
meet the conditions, so

146
00:08:32.195 --> 00:08:37.198
the amount of the healthcare
allowance is not determined

147
00:08:37.198 --> 00:08:42.741
and sensitive data, such as
detention, is not looked at.

148
00:08:42.741 --> 00:08:46.103
So data minimisation is
built in automatically.

149
00:08:47.876 --> 00:08:51.457
Feel free to look around yourself.

150
00:08:53.158 --> 00:08:58.968
Play with the scenarios
and follow the traces.

151
00:08:58.968 --> 00:09:03.837
A question I often get:

152
00:09:03.837 --> 00:09:09.426
How do we get those ruleworks?

153
00:09:09.426 --> 00:09:14.915
Short answer: we use
generative AI to turn laws and

154
00:09:14.915 --> 00:09:18.825
regulations into a rulework.

155
00:09:18.825 --> 00:09:24.147
We also turn the explanatory
memorandum into a

156
00:09:24.147 --> 00:09:25.454
scenario.

157
00:09:25.454 --> 00:09:30.235
Then we run the scenario
and see it's not right yet.

158
00:09:30.235 --> 00:09:33.056
Some things did not pass.

159
00:09:33.056 --> 00:09:38.558
We give that outcome to

160
00:09:38.558 --> 00:09:40.539
the language model for another round.

161
00:09:40.539 --> 00:09:45.200
Out comes a new rulework and scenario.

162
00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:47.641
That is run again,

163
00:09:47.641 --> 00:09:51.966
until everything shows as passed.

164
00:09:51.966 --> 00:09:55.210
Then we hand it to a legal
analyst or legislative lawyer.

165
00:09:55.210 --> 00:10:00.196
That is the starting point
for a lawyer to look at how

166
00:10:00.196 --> 00:10:03.359
the machine-executable laws and regulations
work: does it do what it should,

167
00:10:03.359 --> 00:10:05.121
and does it implement the law

168
00:10:05.121 --> 00:10:08.438
and regulations the way we want?

169
00:10:08.438 --> 00:10:12.782
Once a law is machine-executable,
you can do all sorts of things.

170
00:10:12.782 --> 00:10:14.643
You can also

171
00:10:14.643 --> 00:10:17.646
run it on several people at once.

172
00:10:17.646 --> 00:10:22.389
In the scenarios we ran
the law on one individual.

173
00:10:22.389 --> 00:10:25.952
Here we run it on a population,

174
00:10:25.952 --> 00:10:27.273
in this case 50 people.

175
00:10:28.654 --> 00:10:34.619
Average 54 years, 36% have
a partner, 40% rent, 18%

176
00:10:34.619 --> 00:10:37.942
have children, 4% study,
average income 41,000 euros.

177
00:10:37.942 --> 00:10:43.947
This is a small population

178
00:10:43.947 --> 00:10:49.191
and so not very realistic, but
sampled from the statistics of

179
00:10:49.191 --> 00:10:50.812
Statistics Netherlands (CBS).

180
00:10:50.812 --> 00:10:55.856
Once you have all the legislation
and regulations, which

181
00:10:55.856 --> 00:10:57.601
we see below,

182
00:10:57.601 --> 00:11:01.264
Here we see social assistance,
income-related contribution, ZVW,

183
00:11:01.264 --> 00:11:05.087
Health Insurance Act, income tax, right
to vote, House of Representatives,

184
00:11:05.087 --> 00:11:06.188
and so on.

185
00:11:06.188 --> 00:11:10.331
If you make laws machine-executable,
and run them on such a

186
00:11:10.331 --> 00:11:11.779
population,

187
00:11:11.779 --> 00:11:13.541
you can compute this kind of thing.

188
00:11:13.541 --> 00:11:16.885
Disposable income: what you have
left at the end of the month.

189
00:11:16.885 --> 00:11:22.411
Income minus tax, plus
allowances and benefits.

190
00:11:22.411 --> 00:11:26.235
That is how you also account for
the interaction between laws.

191
00:11:26.235 --> 00:11:28.798
If you get more somewhere, you get

192
00:11:28.798 --> 00:11:29.819
less elsewhere.

193
00:11:29.819 --> 00:11:32.856
You can see that in these amounts.

194
00:11:32.856 --> 00:11:36.539
If you make laws executable, you can

195
00:11:36.539 --> 00:11:40.943
look at the effect of legislative changes.

196
00:11:40.943 --> 00:11:46.481
For example, a change to the default
premium of the healthcare allowance.

197
00:11:46.481 --> 00:11:50.985
We more or less double
it and run a simulation,

198
00:11:50.985 --> 00:11:56.669
on the same population.
Then we see what happens

199
00:11:56.669 --> 00:11:57.610
and we can compare.

200
00:11:57.610 --> 00:12:01.741
Here you see the comparison
between the default situation

201
00:12:01.741 --> 00:12:04.842
and the situation with the
amended default premium.

202
00:12:04.842 --> 00:12:09.537
In this case we see that

203
00:12:09.537 --> 00:12:11.638
the disposable income per month

204
00:12:12.958 --> 00:12:18.280
rises from 4720 to 4775 euros.

205
00:12:19.321 --> 00:12:21.942
If you sit in the House of
Representatives and have to vote

206
00:12:21.942 --> 00:12:26.744
on an amendment to the default premium,

207
00:12:26.744 --> 00:12:27.824
it helps to have this kind of

208
00:12:29.039 --> 00:12:33.761
Healthcare allowance is the
effect of a change in the law.

209
00:12:33.761 --> 00:12:35.662
We see that the

210
00:12:35.662 --> 00:12:36.943
Healthcare Allowance Act

211
00:12:36.943 --> 00:12:42.665
goes up considerably

212
00:12:42.665 --> 00:12:43.405
.

213
00:12:43.405 --> 00:12:47.467
Elsewhere, people are apparently
being cut. You want to know that.

214
00:12:48.766 --> 00:12:54.010
Feel free to click around here,
try other changes in the law

215
00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:58.433
and make different comparisons.

216
00:12:58.433 --> 00:13:02.535
You can also zoom in on the
breakdown within the population.

217
00:13:02.535 --> 00:13:07.719
Here you see the breakdown by age, income,

218
00:13:07.719 --> 00:13:13.403
partner, renting or buying,

219
00:13:13.403 --> 00:13:14.103
and children.

220
00:13:15.378 --> 00:13:17.219
Feel free to look around.

221
00:13:17.219 --> 00:13:19.100
We'll continue shortly.

222
00:13:20.387 --> 00:13:24.889
If you make laws machine-executable,
this is possible too.

223
00:13:24.889 --> 00:13:29.752
This is a kind of My government.

224
00:13:31.012 --> 00:13:35.794
I'll reset the demo for a moment.

225
00:13:35.794 --> 00:13:36.975
This is a

226
00:13:40.499 --> 00:13:42.501
portal for Merijn.

227
00:13:42.501 --> 00:13:44.523
There is also a portal for
Claudia, an entrepreneur.

228
00:13:44.523 --> 00:13:48.567
Merijn should be entitled to

229
00:13:48.567 --> 00:13:51.990
child budget, healthcare allowance,
social assistance from the municipality

230
00:13:51.990 --> 00:13:53.691
of Amsterdam, and so on.

231
00:13:53.691 --> 00:13:56.414
Here you see all the earlier laws again.

232
00:13:56.414 --> 00:14:00.137
Take the healthcare allowance: here we see

233
00:14:00.137 --> 00:14:03.701
the trace from just now, but
only the data points from that

234
00:14:03.701 --> 00:14:03.962
trace.

235
00:14:03.962 --> 00:14:08.066
Merijn is not in detention, for example.

236
00:14:08.066 --> 00:14:10.229
That comes from the
Penitentiary Principles Act.

237
00:14:10.229 --> 00:14:14.474
Merijn sees the data, finds it
correct and submits an application.

238
00:14:14.474 --> 00:14:16.739
It is granted straight away.

239
00:14:16.739 --> 00:14:20.402
Dienst Toeslagen, the
competent authority for the

240
00:14:20.402 --> 00:14:24.505
Healthcare Allowance Act,
grants this amount directly.

241
00:14:24.505 --> 00:14:30.489
That works because the application
used machine-executable legislation

242
00:14:30.489 --> 00:14:35.032
for the healthcare allowance,
and Dienst Toeslagen uses exactly

243
00:14:35.032 --> 00:14:37.474
the same machine-executable
legislation at the back end.

244
00:14:37.474 --> 00:14:42.858
So this is not the calculator,
but the real interpretation of

245
00:14:42.858 --> 00:14:43.538
law and regulation.

246
00:14:44.062 --> 00:14:45.703
The housing benefit works
slightly differently.

247
00:14:45.703 --> 00:14:51.727
Merijn has to supply data,
such as the rent and eligible

248
00:14:51.727 --> 00:14:54.349
service costs.

249
00:14:54.349 --> 00:14:56.991
An amount comes out: 302.96 euros.

250
00:14:56.991 --> 00:15:01.154
Merijn perhaps expected a bit more.

251
00:15:01.154 --> 00:15:03.492
On closer inspection he also sees:

252
00:15:03.492 --> 00:15:05.673
his income is completely wrong.

253
00:15:05.673 --> 00:15:08.655
He clicks through and sees
profit from a business.

254
00:15:08.655 --> 00:15:14.538
Last year Merijn may have had
a business, but he no longer

255
00:15:14.538 --> 00:15:17.460
has one.

256
00:15:17.460 --> 00:15:20.161
Perhaps with a deregistration at the KVK.

257
00:15:20.161 --> 00:15:22.323
Merijn submits that.

258
00:15:22.323 --> 00:15:23.443
Here we see the change.

259
00:15:23.443 --> 00:15:27.546
The amount goes up straight
away, from 302 to 406.95 euros.

260
00:15:29.906 --> 00:15:34.050
Merijn can apply for this, and
it may need to be put under

261
00:15:34.050 --> 00:15:34.450
assessment.

262
00:15:34.450 --> 00:15:40.455
As the government, we may want to
look at the change in Merijn's income

263
00:15:40.455 --> 00:15:44.019
and the proof of
deregistration at the KVK.

264
00:15:45.112 --> 00:15:49.916
The healthcare allowance that was
already applied for and granted

265
00:15:49.916 --> 00:15:53.760
is no longer correct.

266
00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:57.764
That allowance was based
on income, and Merijn

267
00:15:57.764 --> 00:15:59.165
has changed that.

268
00:15:59.165 --> 00:16:03.509
So Merijn may also want to amend that
application, and it may also need

269
00:16:03.509 --> 00:16:08.333
to be put under assessment, because the
supporting document, the deregistration

270
00:16:08.333 --> 00:16:11.216
at the KVK, still has to be
reviewed by a civil servant.

271
00:16:11.910 --> 00:16:17.435
Here people often ask
whether this could not have

272
00:16:17.435 --> 00:16:17.996
been done proactively.

273
00:16:19.161 --> 00:16:24.746
This demo grew out of a proactive
service delivery fieldlab, in December

274
00:16:24.746 --> 00:16:25.466
2024.

275
00:16:27.428 --> 00:16:30.250
That was the reason to
start working on this.

276
00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:35.214
If we can compute the right to
healthcare allowance and its amount

277
00:16:35.214 --> 00:16:39.778
with machine-executable laws
and regulations, we can

278
00:16:39.778 --> 00:16:43.281
why not grant it proactively right away?

279
00:16:43.281 --> 00:16:44.081
The short answer:

280
00:16:45.402 --> 00:16:51.031
technically maybe, but
it's not always desirable,

281
00:16:51.031 --> 00:16:53.996
because we don't know everything
about the applications.

282
00:16:55.831 --> 00:17:01.797
The longer answer: to grant straight
away, we would need to know everything

283
00:17:01.797 --> 00:17:03.999
about an applicant.

284
00:17:03.999 --> 00:17:06.581
For example, whether someone has
just received an inheritance.

285
00:17:06.581 --> 00:17:11.205
With all the government's data, it looks

286
00:17:11.205 --> 00:17:15.028
as if you're entitled to healthcare allowance,
but the government doesn't know everything.

287
00:17:15.028 --> 00:17:19.933
For example, if you've just
received an inheritance,

288
00:17:20.872 --> 00:17:25.056
that affects your entitlement to
healthcare allowance and its amount.

289
00:17:25.056 --> 00:17:29.079
If we granted that straight away,

290
00:17:29.079 --> 00:17:31.181
you could end up in trouble
and have to repay everything.

291
00:17:31.181 --> 00:17:32.943
That requires choices.

292
00:17:32.943 --> 00:17:38.027
Political choices, and
perhaps legislative changes,

293
00:17:38.027 --> 00:17:40.509
to be able to do this reliably.

294
00:17:42.237 --> 00:17:46.942
Merijn has made these two applications.

295
00:17:46.942 --> 00:17:51.768
There is a case system,
here for Dienst Toeslagen,

296
00:17:51.768 --> 00:17:54.323
but it could also be another organisation.

297
00:17:54.323 --> 00:17:58.025
Dienst Toeslagen sees
Merijn's applications:

298
00:17:58.025 --> 00:17:58.625
housing benefit and healthcare
allowance, and can review them.

299
00:17:58.625 --> 00:18:03.587
Merijn has made an amendment here.

300
00:18:03.587 --> 00:18:06.248
Profit from a business
went from 25,000 to zero.

301
00:18:06.248 --> 00:18:08.209
That can be approved or rejected.

302
00:18:08.209 --> 00:18:12.711
Or the whole application
is granted or rejected.

303
00:18:12.711 --> 00:18:18.694
This civil servant weighs
this carefully and explains

304
00:18:18.694 --> 00:18:21.735
why this is wrong, and rejects it.

305
00:18:24.006 --> 00:18:26.607
For Merijn, that looks like this.

306
00:18:26.607 --> 00:18:31.048
His housing benefit application has
been rejected and he can object.

307
00:18:33.609 --> 00:18:37.851
Objecting is yet another law, namely the

308
00:18:37.851 --> 00:18:39.451
General Administrative Law Act.

309
00:18:39.451 --> 00:18:45.353
The General Administrative Law
Act is machine-executable too.

310
00:18:45.353 --> 00:18:46.814
We can look at that as well.

311
00:18:46.814 --> 00:18:49.975
Here we see the objection.

312
00:18:51.112 --> 00:18:53.354
And when you can object.

313
00:18:53.354 --> 00:18:57.537
With the healthcare allowance,
the amount was an output,

314
00:18:57.537 --> 00:19:01.388
here, being able to object is an output.

315
00:19:01.388 --> 00:19:06.031
The law the decision
came from must not be a

316
00:19:06.031 --> 00:19:08.614
generally binding regulation,
policy rule, preparatory decision

317
00:19:08.614 --> 00:19:11.476
or other act, but must be a
decision or have a decision of

318
00:19:11.476 --> 00:19:12.877
general application as its result.

319
00:19:13.695 --> 00:19:19.206
And you must not have objected before.
So you see that procedural legislation,

320
00:19:19.206 --> 00:19:24.050
such as this part of the Awb,

321
00:19:24.050 --> 00:19:27.313
can also be made machine-executable.

322
00:19:27.313 --> 00:19:29.034
And that it can take part.

323
00:19:29.034 --> 00:19:34.098
The 'objection possible'
block sits in this

324
00:19:34.098 --> 00:19:36.220
user interface, in this piece of software.

325
00:19:36.220 --> 00:19:41.203
But the business logic, which
determines whether someone

326
00:19:41.203 --> 00:19:45.405
can object, sits in such a rulework.

327
00:19:45.405 --> 00:19:51.308
That way a piece of the Awb only has to be

328
00:19:51.308 --> 00:19:54.610
interpreted and made
machine-executable once

329
00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:56.475
to make it work everywhere.

330
00:19:56.475 --> 00:20:00.858
The laws and regulations we see here.

331
00:20:00.858 --> 00:20:04.280
That applies not only to the Awb,
but also to other procedural laws and

332
00:20:04.280 --> 00:20:10.164
regulations, such as the
Archives Act, the GDPR or the

333
00:20:10.164 --> 00:20:13.787
Woo, or parts of those laws.

334
00:20:13.787 --> 00:20:18.130
So you can go far in making procedural

335
00:20:18.130 --> 00:20:19.766
laws and regulations machine-executable.

336
00:20:21.262 --> 00:20:22.823
I want to stop here.

337
00:20:22.823 --> 00:20:25.225
That was RegelRecht: from
law to digital effect.

338
00:20:25.225 --> 00:20:27.566
A complete story, not a finished product.

339
00:20:27.566 --> 00:20:30.168
We make policy by doing.

340
00:20:30.168 --> 00:20:31.589
The demo is completely open.

341
00:20:31.589 --> 00:20:33.150
You can try it yourself.

342
00:20:33.150 --> 00:20:35.752
Click 'try it yourself'
or just click around.

343
00:20:35.752 --> 00:20:40.115
Read more at regelrecht.rijks.app.
